    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d89">

                <pb facs="0269" n="243" xml:id="pg243"/>

                <head>89. Memorandum From R.C.
                        Bowman of the National Security Council Staff to the Executive
                    Secretary of the National Security Council (Smith)<anchor n="1" xml:id="d89-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1965-05-24">May 24, 1965</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT</label>
                    <item>National Command System</item>
                </list>
                <p>I have attached two JCS papers that you might
                    like to scan. I have not heard any more about the command system study since I
                    spoke to you about it in February.</p>
                <p>
                    JCSM 129<anchor n="2" xml:id="d89-f2"/>
                    indicates the Chiefs’ general agreement with the study with the exception that
                    they felt it underrates the Alternate Command Center at Fort Ritchie. At that
                    time the Chiefs deferred judgment on the Command Post Afloat.</p>
                <p>In the second paper, JCSM 364 (17 May),<anchor
                        n="3" xml:id="d89-f3"/> they concluded that two command ships are essential,
                    and that the capabilities of the USS <hi rendition="#italic">Northampton</hi>
                    should be improved. The Chief of Naval Operations disagreed, and argued that one
                    ship was sufficient.</p>
                <p>In the last analysis, the value of any command facility must be determined to a
                    great extent by the probability that the President will, in fact, make use of
                    that facility.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#bold">RCB</hi>
                        <anchor n="4" xml:id="d89-f4"/>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d89-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d89-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Johnson Library, National Security File, Agency File, JCS, Filed by the LBJ Library, Box 29. Top
                    Secret.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d89-f2" n="2" xml:id="d89-f2-content">
                <p>
                    JCSM–129–65, February 26, “Conceptual Approach
                    to the National Military Command System”; not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d89-f3" n="3" xml:id="d89-f3-content">
                <p>
                    JCSM–364–65, “National Emergency Command Post
                    Afloat”; not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d89-f4" n="4" xml:id="d89-f4-content">
                <p>A typed note under Bowman’s initials
                    reads: “Please return.” Bowman wrote a
                    note at the bottom of the page: “It is long overdue that we take a positive hand
                    in this &amp; some other related command control matters. RCB”</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
